behaviour BEH-STREAK-3 "Longest streak ignores duplicate and out-of-order dates"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.Habits.Tests/HabitCalculatorTests.cs:LongestStreak_ignores_duplicate_and_unordered_dates
  review unreviewed
  serves BEH-HISTORY-1
  actor owner
  contract duplicate and unordered entry dates do not inflate the longest streak

behaviour BEH-ENTRY-1 "Logging the same habit twice on one day overwrites rather than appends"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.Habits.Tests/HabitServiceTests.cs:UpsertEntry_inserts_then_overwrites_the_same_day
  review unreviewed
  serves BEH-TODAY-2
  serves BEH-TODAY-4
  actor owner
  contract PUT /api/entries is an upsert keyed on (habitId, date)

# CORRECTED SINCE #3. This was filed as an inference that contradicted the doc.
# It is neither: DESIGN.md's Architecture section documents the parameter, so the
# behaviour is DEFINED — and what the code and the doc actually disagree about is
# the parameter's NAME. The doc says `?days=30`; HabitRoutes.cs:68 binds
# `int historyDays = 30`. A frontend written from the design document would send
# `?days=7`, get no error, and silently render 30 days. Nothing in the repo
# catches that: the backend test calls the handler, not the query string.
behaviour BEH-HISTORY-2 "The history window length is requested by the caller, defaulting to 30"
  source defined docs/DESIGN.md#architecture-api
  actor owner
  contract GET /api/habits/{id}/history takes a window-length parameter defaulting to 30

behaviour BEH-HISTORY-3 "The history window parameter is named historyDays, not days"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.Habits.WebApi/Routes/HabitRoutes.cs:68
  review unreviewed
  serves BEH-HISTORY-1
  actor owner
  contract the query parameter is `historyDays`, and `?days=` is silently ignored

behaviour BEH-REORDER-1 "Habits can be reordered as a whole list in one call"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.Habits.WebApi/Routes/HabitRoutes.cs:ReorderHabits
  review unreviewed
  serves BEH-MANAGE-1
  actor owner
  contract PUT /api/habits/reorder rewrites every sort position

# NO `serves` LINE either, and it is a DIFFERENT case from BEH-ARCHIVE-2 — the
# report says "nothing documented displays it" and stops there deliberately,
# because the two need opposite answers. This is inherited web-template
# behaviour, not habits surface: it is real and worth keeping, and what is
# missing is a documented error state on the Today screen for the frontend to
# render it into. Two behaviours can share a finding and not share a fix; a tool
# that collapsed them into one recommendation would be wrong half the time.
behaviour BEH-ERROR-1 "An unknown habit is a 404 carrying a machine-readable error code"
  source inferred backend/Balenthiran.Habits.Tests/ProblemDetailsResponseTests.cs:Not_found_returns_a_problem_details_body_with_error_code
  review unreviewed
  actor owner
  contract GET, PUT, DELETE and history for an unknown habit id all return 404
  contract the body is ProblemDetails and carries an error code
  contract an unexpected exception is a 500 that exposes nothing
  asks "Nothing documented displays an error. Should DESIGN.md gain an error state the frontend renders these into, or should the machine-readable code go and a status code be the whole contract?"
  option "Document the error state" "DESIGN.md gains a row saying what each screen shows when a habit 404s and when the API 500s. The error code earns a purpose — the frontend maps code to copy. Costs a design edit now, and commits three screens that do not exist yet to per-code messages."
  option "Drop the code, keep the status" "ProblemDetails stays, the bespoke code goes, and one backend test simplifies. The frontend shows one generic failure. You lose the ability to tell 'this habit was archived' apart from 'that id was never real' without adding the code back later."
  recommend "Document the error state" "A 404 here is not an exotic path — it is a direct consequence of A5. DELETE archives rather than deletes, so any list the client is holding can name an id the backend now refuses. Undocumented means the frontend author invents the behaviour at the keyboard, which is the thing this corpus exists to stop."
  against "A6 says single user, and a single user on a single device reaches that stale-id race close to never — so the case above is largely hypothetical, and it is being used to add scope to an app whose Today screen is not built. There is also a cheaper middle this question does not offer: document ONE generic error state, leave the code in the payload unconsumed, and decide per-code copy on the day a second code actually distinguishes something. Picking the full version now spends design effort on branches that may never be rendered."


# ═══ THE UNKNOWNS MECHANISM, ON REAL MATERIAL ═══
#
# BEH-TODAY-3 says "fills the value field" and glosses over what a valid value
# IS. That hole is not answered by asking the author — it is answered by the
# completion rule, which a different part of the design already states (A3), and
# which the calculator tests already enforce.

behaviour BEH-COMPLETE-1 "Completion is defined per habit type"
  source defined docs/DESIGN.md#A3
  actor owner
  provides field:HabitValue.value = 1
  contract boolean habits complete when ticked
  contract numeric habits with a target complete at or above the target
  contract numeric habits without a target complete when any value is entered

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A DELIBERATE CONFLICT, LEFT IN — AND CORRECTED SINCE #3.
#
# #3 reported this as doc-vs-code: "DESIGN.md promises a 30-day grid, the shipped
# route takes a caller-supplied window". Checked line by line, that is WRONG in
# the direction that made it sound better. The route is
# `int historyDays = 30` (HabitRoutes.cs:68) — caller-supplied with a default of
# 30, which satisfies "30-day grid" — and DESIGN.md's own Architecture section
# already documents `GET /api/habits/{id}/history?days=30`.
#
# So both sides are DEFINED, in the same document, three sections apart: MVP 5
# fixes the window, Architecture parameterises it. That is a smaller drama and a
# better finding, because spec-vs-spec is the one axis Kiro's spec-to-property
# testing does not cover — it measures spec against code.
#
# Kit's job here is to name the collision, not to resolve it. A default of 30 may
# be exactly what he meant; it may also be how a "30-day grid" quietly becomes a
# 7-day one. Both sides are printed; the choice is his.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

behaviour BEH-WINDOW-MVP "The history window is 30 days"
  source defined docs/DESIGN.md#mvp-5
  actor owner
  provides region:CompletionGrid.days = 30

behaviour BEH-WINDOW-API "The history window is whatever the caller asks for"
  source defined docs/DESIGN.md#architecture-api
  actor owner
  provides region:CompletionGrid.days = caller-supplied
  cites BEH-HISTORY-3
  asks "The two sides only contradict each other on paper — the route is caller-supplied DEFAULTING to 30, which satisfies both. The real collision underneath is the NAME: DESIGN.md documents ?days=30, the route binds ?historyDays. Which one is the contract the frontend gets built against?"
  option "Rename the code to days" "HabitRoutes.cs:68 binds `int days = 30` and HabitApiTests.cs:182 follows. A frontend written from DESIGN.md then works. It is a breaking query-parameter change to a route, and the generated API client needs regenerating."
  option "Rename the design to historyDays" "DESIGN.md:83 becomes ?historyDays=30. No code moves, no client regenerates, nothing can break. The document a human and an assistant read to build the screen gets the uglier of the two names."
  recommend "Rename the code to days" "This is the cheapest it will ever be: the history screen is habits#13, unbuilt, so there is no consumer to break today and exactly one after it lands. And your own frontend-first rule points this way — the parameter is named by what the screen asks for, not by a handler's local variable. The response body already returns a `days` array, so ?days=7 returning seven days reads as one idea rather than two."
  against "Nothing is actually broken right now: the default is 30 either way, so the only caller that gets hurt is one written from the doc that also passes the parameter, and none exists. Editing one line of prose is a strictly smaller change than editing a route, a test and a regenerated client, and it carries no chance of breaking a deploy. `historyDays` is also the more explicit name at the call site — `?days` sitting next to a `days` array in the response can read as though it filters that field rather than sizing it. And 'rename it now while it is free' is the argument that gets made immediately before the first consumer appears."
